    {
      "id": "crossref:10.1177/01956574261493404",
      "doi": "10.1177/01956574261493404",
      "title": "Editor’s Introduction: Bridging Research and Practice: Policy Insights for the Evolving Electricity Sector",
      "abstract": "This special issue collects six articles that translate economic research into practical guidance for electricity regulators. The articles cover regulated monopoly structure, the economics of regulation after restructuring, electric vehicle integration in constrained distribution networks, distributed backup power and grid reliability, the spatial and temporal variation in the environmental value of renewable energy, and energy justice. Across these domains, legacy regulatory frameworks designed for an earlier technological era now generate predictable distortions. Three themes recur: institutional lag, or the pacing problem; the dispersed and tacit knowledge that no central authority can fully aggregate; and the tension between efficiency and equity. Together, the contributions show that economic analysis can inform the decisions regulators face, provided researchers write for practitioners and practitioners' institutional experience shapes the questions researchers ask. JEL Classification : L90, Industry Studies: Transportation and Utilities: General; L51, Economics of Regulation; H23, Taxation and Subsidies: Externalities; Redistributive Effects; Environmental Taxes and Subsidies",
